 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Time complexity is $O(m \times n)$ for all four metrics, as each requires a single pass or two passes over the image pixels. Memory complexity is $O(1)$ auxiliary space (excluding the input image and histogram storage if $L$ is large).
-*   **Performance:** The metrics are computationally inexpensive and can be calculated immediately after rendering.
-*   **Strengths:** No reference image is required, making it suitable for scenarios where ground truth is unavailable or difficult to obtain [S1, S2]. It provides quantitative data on visual properties like chaos and clarity.
-*   **Limitations:** The interpretation of $U$ and $\text{Std}$ is context-dependent; larger values are not universally "better" [S1]. The method does not account for perceptual factors that PSNR or SSIM might capture via reference comparison. The definition of gradients and entropy base lacks standardization in the source [S2].
-*   **Usage:** Particularly useful in explosion field visualization to evaluate the reduction of visual occlusion and the clarity of microstructures like shock waves [S1, S2].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(m \cdot n)$ for all four metrics, as each requires a single pass (or two passes for Mean/Std) over the image pixels.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space if computed in a streaming fashion, or $O(L)$ for the histogram in the entropy calculation.
+- **Strengths:** No reference image required; computationally inexpensive; provides objective numerical feedback on visual properties like clarity (Gradient) and chaos (Entropy) [S1, S2].
+- **Limitations:** Subjective interpretation of "quality" depends heavily on the visualization goal (e.g., high vs. low mean) [S1]. It does not account for structural similarity or perceptual fidelity in the same way SSIM does [S2].
+- **When to use:** When ground truth is unavailable, or for rapid feedback during parameter tuning in volume rendering (e.g., transfer function optimization) [S1, S2].
+- **When not to use:** When perceptual accuracy relative to a known standard is critical, or when structural preservation is the primary metric of interest [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of volume rendering visualization for explosion field data [S1, S2]. It is referred to as the "first evaluation method" in the source literature [S1, S2]. The approach was applied to evaluate rendering results where ideal reference images were difficult to obtain, such as in simulations of shock waves and detonation products [S1, S2]. Specific results showed improvements in Mean Gradient (e.g., 12% to 43% increases in certain groups) and reductions in Image Mean and Standard Deviation (up to 28.38% and 34.88% respectively) when using optimized visualization methods [S1].
+The reference-free method, also known as the "first evaluation method," was widely adopted in volume rendering visualization research because obtaining ideal reference images is generally difficult [S1, S2]. It is based on image statistical properties and typically includes four indices: Image Entropy ($H$), Mean Gradient ($G$), Image Mean ($U$), and Standard Deviation ($\text{Std}$) [S1, S2]. The method has been applied to evaluate explosion field data visualization, where it helps quantify the visibility of shock waves and jets by reducing visual occlusion [S1]. In such studies, improvements in microstructure clarity were measured by increases in Mean Gradient (e.g., 12% to 43% increases in specific groups) [S1].
 
